  "query_preview": "..\nScattered weak showers with isolated thunder have\nformed over the last hour between Nogales and Benson. This may\nbe the result of outflows moving in from Sonora from convection\nWednesday evening. Expect shower activity to persist for a \nfew more hours with occasional lightning. \n\nAloft we are in a transitional monsoon pattern with ridging \nwell to the east and a closed low over the Pacific Northwest. \nPWATs have increased by about a 0.25\" over the last 24 hours \nfrom central Pima County eastward. This is in part due to \nsoutherly flow over Arizona and Sonora pulling up tropical \nmoisture from Hurricane Marie. Because of this expect \nthunderstorm coverage to expand northward compared to Wednesday \nwith 30-60 PoPs from the Tohono O'Odham Nation eastward. CAMs \nshow convection starting as early as 11 AM and continuing \nthrough the evening hours. The main threat looks to be heavy \nrainfall thanks to the above normal precipitable water. The \n03/00Z HREF paints areas from southern Pinal County down to \nSanta Cruz County and the eastern half of Graham and Cochise \nCounties as having the best chances for 1\" hourly rainfall \nrates this afternoon and evening. This could lead to areas of \nflash flooding and also rises in area washes and rivers. Friday \nthe best moisture shifts slightly east with the best chances \nfor scattered thunderstorms from Tucson eastward. Again, the \nmain threat will be heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding. \nBoth today and Friday the WPC has portions of southeast Arizona \nunder a slight risk for excessive rainfall. \n\nThis weekend into early next week the upper air pattern remains\nmostly unchanged with a closed low over the Pacific Northwest\nand high pressure over the Great Plains and Midwest. This will\nallow tropical moisture to continue to advect into the region.\nExpect daily afternoon and evening thunderstorms capable of\nheavy rainfall leading to flash flooding. After Monday the\nPacific Northwest low begins to move inland, but is immediately\nreplaced by another trough over the West Coast. However there\nare large differences between ensemble systems in how strong the\nsecond upper low will be. Models do agree though that PWATs \nwill be 25-50 percent above normal through at least next week \nThursday, so expect continued daily afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms past Monday.\n\nTemperatures will be near or just slightly below normal through the \nforecast period.",
